        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "75309939-f801-5a69-a6c2-370dc860dafe",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Paul Hindemith, Gioachino Rossini, Andrea Leone Tottola, Sir Walter Scott, Maria de Francesca-Cavazza, Wolfgang Sawallisch\n- Works: Cardillac, La donna del lago, The Lady of the Lake\n- Organizations: Deutsche Grammophon\n- Roles: Cardillac's daughter\n\nFacts:\n- In 1810, Sir Walter Scott wrote the narrative poem The Lady of the Lake.\n- La donna del lago is an opera composed by Gioachino Rossini with a libretto by Andrea Leone Tottola based on the French translation of Sir Walter Scott's The Lady of the Lake.\n- Cardillac is an opera by Paul Hindemith in three acts and four scenes.\n- Maria de Francesca-Cavazza appears as Cardillac's daughter in Paul Hindemith's opera Cardillac on a 1985 Munich DVD conducted by Wolfgang Sawallisch and issued by Deutsche Grammophon.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "e7a2ad15-7ba3-5178-8fe4-bcad9ccbfe29",
        "text": "Page: Cardillac. Sentence: Cardillac is an opera by Paul Hindemith in three acts and four scenes.\nPage: La donna del lago. Sentence: La donna del lago (The Lady of the Lake) is an opera composed by Gioachino Rossini with a libretto by Andrea Leone Tottola (whose verses are described as \"limpid\" by one critic) based on the French translation of \"The Lady of the Lake\", a narrative poem written in 1810 by Sir Walter Scott, whose work continued to popularize the image of the romantic highlands.\nPage: Maria de Francesca-Cavazza. Sentence: She can be heard and seen in the role of the Cardillac's daughter in Hindemith's opera Cardillac, conducted by Wolfgang Sawallisch, on a 1985 Munich DVD issued by Deutsche Grammophon.",
